  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">From Margins to Leadership</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emerging democracies in Africa face a paradox such as effective civic participation requires a motivated electorate but to be motivated, the electorates need to believe their participation will be effective otherwise, why show up on an election day?. Building motivation is a particular problem for young Africans and Zambian youth. Youth in Zambia are hardly involved in decision making nor politically empowered aside from being used as tools during campaigns and voting hence stagnation in development.

From Margins to Leadership is a 15 months project aimed at empowering youth with non-formal education and Practically integrates the indigenous rural youth who are economically and socially disadvantaged, persons with disabilities, from Northern, Muchinga, Luapula and Southern Provinces with human rights, access to information through the access to information act of 2024, and advocacy strategies not only to effectively amplify youth rights, but also find or a seat and participate in the planning and implementation of the constituency development fund, National development plans in line ministries and at SADC and African union to ensure they create the change they want tosee in the their communities.

This shall Increase the participation rural young people in decision making processes, policy reviews and formulation to contribute to a broader and more developed Zambia and an effective Zambian youth policy, the vision 2030 and agenda 2063 to benefit marginalized young people from typical rural communities, hence driving change in their communities.</narrative>
  </description>
